A useful quantitative estimate for the general κ-noncollapsing argument: Perelman's proof uses that the reduced volume V(τ) of flat ℝⁿ equals 1, and more specifically the estimate that a ball of radius √(4πτ)... Hmm. Actually, in Perelman's argument, one key quantitative step is: V(τ) ≤ ∫ over B(q, r) of (4πτ)^{-n/2} e^{-l} + small error, and on B(q,r) one has l ≤ C, giving a lower bound μ(B(q,r)) ≥ V(τ) · (4πτ)^{n/2} e^{-C}. A genuinely useful model-space estimate in this spirit: **the Gaussian normalization restricted to a ball**: ∫_{B(0, r)} (4πτ)^{-n/2} e^{-|q|²/(4τ)} dq ≥ ... or ≤ ... This would be a "quantitative estimate required for the general argument": in the flat model, the reduced volume concentrates on the ball of radius ~ √τ.

Alternatively, an even more relevant quantitative estimate: since exp(-|q|²/(4τ)) ≤ 1 on B(0,r), ∫_{B(0,r)} (4πτ)^{-n/2} exp(-|q|²/4τ) dq ≤ vol(B(0,r))·(4πτ)^{-n/2}, and dually the mass outside a ball is bounded: ∫_{|q|≥r} (4πτ)^{-n/2} e^{-|q|²/(4τ)} dq ≤ exp(-r²/(4τ))·... hmm since on the complement exp(-|q|²/(4τ)) ≤ exp(-r²/(4τ)): mass outside ≤ (4πτ)^{-n/2} · exp(-r²/(4τ)) · ∫_{|q|≥r} 1 = 0 (infinite measure)... not integrable. Better: tail bound via Markov/Chebyshev — ∫_{|q|≥r} density ≤ exp(-r²/(4τ)) · ∫ (4πτ)^{-n/2} exp(-|q|²/(8τ)) dq. Hmm, that's again a Gaussian integral with a different τ: ∫ e^{-|q|²/(8τ)} = (8πτ)^{n/2}. So mass outside B(0,r) ≤ e^{-r²/(4τ)} (8πτ)^{n/2}/(4πτ)^{n/2} = 2^{n/2} e^{-r²/(4τ)}. That's a genuinely quantitative Gaussian tail bound — the "mass concentrates near the origin" statement. This is a classic quantitative estimate used in blow-up arguments. With r = √(C n τ) this gives mass outside ≤ 2^{n/2} e^{-Cn/4} → small.

These would be genuine new quantitative estimates. Let me think about what's provable in the remaining time with mathlib. The tail bound needs: split integral over {|q| ≥ r} vs whole space: ∫ over set ≤ ∫ over whole space of nonneg function — that's `set_integral_le_integral` or `integral_mono_on`... For nonnegative functions, ∫_{s} f ≤ ∫_{univ} f: use `MeasureTheory.set_integral_le_integral` requires... Actually simpler: ∫_{s} f = ∫ indicator_s f ≤ ∫ f pointwise (indicator·f ≤ f when f ≥ 0). There's `integral_mono_ae`/`integral_mono`. And pointwise on the complement: exp(-|q|²/(4τ)) ≤ exp(-r²/(4τ)) for |q| ≥ r since exp is monotone and -|q|²/(4τ) ≤ -r²/(4τ) when |q| ≥ r (with 4τ > 0). Then ∫_s f ≤ exp(-r²/(4τ)) ∫_s e^{-|q|²/(8τ)}? Wait I need f ≤ c·g pointwise with g = e^{-|q|²/(8τ)}: f = e^{-|q|²/(4τ)} = e^{-|q|²/(8τ)}·e^{-|q|²/(8τ)} ≤ e^{-r²/(4τ)}·e^{-|q|²/(8τ)} on the complement (the second factor e^{-|q|²/(8τ)}... wait let me redo: e^{-|q|²/(4τ)} = e^{-|q|²/(8τ) - |q|²/(8τ)} = e^{-|q|²/(8τ)} · e^{-|q|²/(8τ)} ≤ e^{-|q|²/(8τ)} · e^{-r²/(8τ)}. Hmm that gives e^{-r²/(8τ)}. Let me just do the split half/half: e^{-|q|²/(4τ)} = e^{-|q|²/(8τ)} · e^{-|q|²/(8τ)}; on {|q| ≥ r}: second factor ≤ e^{-r²/(8τ)}. So ∫_s f ≤ e^{-r²/(8τ)} ∫ e^{-|q|²/(8τ)} = e^{-r²/(8τ)} (8πτ)^{n/2}. Multiply by (4πτ)^{-n/2}: ≤ e^{-r²/(8τ)} · 2^{n/2}. Good, that's the bound: mass outside B(0,r) ≤ 2^{n/2} e^{-r²/(8τ)}.

